userStories.description:
**Goal** — Give branch review, re-review, fix and repo gates a narrow context and support changes without a PRD.

**Motivation** — A PR opened by a person has no PRD.

**Approach** — **G4 is a type narrowing plus one new provider, not a rewrite.** The four branch steps keep their bodies. Only their parameter type changes, from `FeatureContext` to `BranchStepContext`. `BranchStepContext` lists only the fields those bodies read today, with the same names and types, except `prdPath`, which becomes optional. So a `FeatureContext` is assignable to it with no adapter, and every call inside `nax run` passes the same object it passes today. The G0 equivalence suites (`feature-machine-equivalence.test.ts`, `story-loop-equivalence.test.ts`) and the existing step tests are the proof that nothing changed. They must pass with no edit.

New file `packages/nax/src/workflow/branch-step-context.ts`:
```ts
/** What the branch steps read: inside a run FeatureContext provides it, outside a run buildPrStepContext. */
export interface BranchStepContext {
  readonly callCtx: CallContext;
  readonly config: NaxConfig;
  readonly workflow: WorkflowConfig;
  /** The feature name inside a run; `bot-pr-<number>` for a PR. Used in report headings and logs. */
  readonly feature: string;
  /** Repo root (git cwd). */
  readonly projectDir: string;
  /** Where review packages, step artifacts and the report go. */
  readonly runDir: string;
  readonly reportPath: string;
  /** Absent outside a run: the change has no PRD. */
  readonly prdPath?: string;
  /** Repo-relative package dirs for the repo gates; empty = root gates only. */
  readonly packageDirs: readonly string[];
  readonly runSignal?: AbortSignal;
  readonly dryRun: boolean;
}

export interface PrStepRef {
  readonly forge: ForgeKind;
  readonly number: number;
  /** The base commit or ref the review range starts at. */
  readonly base: string;
  /** The PR head commit sha. */
  readonly head: string;
}

export interface PrStepContext extends BranchStepContext {
  readonly pr: PrStepRef;
}
```

`BranchReviewPromptInput.prdPath` becomes optional. When it is set the prompt is byte-identical to today. When it is absent, the PRD line reads:
```
- the PRD (stories and acceptance criteria): (no PRD for this change)
```

`buildWorkflowBranchFixPrompt`'s `prdPath` becomes optional. Its opening "Read ..." sentence becomes:
| specPath | prdPath | sentence |
|---|---|---|
| set | set | `Read the spec (<spec>) and the PRD (<prd>) for what the feature must do.` (today's text) |
| absent | set | `Read the PRD (<prd>) for what the feature must do.` (today's text) |
| set | absent | `Read the spec (<spec>) for what the feature must do.` |
| absent | absent | the sentence is left out; the prompt goes from the first sentence's `only re-checks what is still open.` straight to the blank line and `## Findings to fix` |

The re-review prompt (`buildWorkflowBranchReReviewPrompt`) has no PRD input and is unchanged.

Changed. The baseline only locates the code; the target is the interface to implement:
- `runBranchReview` (`workflow/branch-review.ts`) - Baseline: `runBranchReview(fc: FeatureContext, input: {...})` - Target: `runBranchReview(fc: BranchStepContext, input: {...})`, body unchanged; passes `fc.prdPath` through to the prompt only when it is set.
- `runBranchReReview` - Baseline `(fc: FeatureContext, ...)`; Target `(fc: BranchStepContext, ...)`.
- `writeFeatureArtifact` - Baseline `(fc: FeatureContext, name, value)`; Target `(fc: BranchStepContext, ...)`.
- `branchReviewModel` - Baseline `(fc: FeatureContext)`; Target `(fc: BranchStepContext)`.
- The private helpers `appendReviewRules` and `appendProjectContextOverride` (`workflow/branch-review.ts`) - Baseline `(fc: FeatureContext, ...)`; Target `(fc: BranchStepContext, ...)`, bodies unchanged.
- `runBranchFix` (`workflow/branch-fix.ts`) - Baseline `(fc: FeatureContext, input)`; Target `(fc: BranchStepContext, input)`; the private helpers `ensureReport`, `commitLeftovers`, `runFixSession` take `BranchStepContext` too.
- `runRepoGates` (`workflow/repo-gates.ts`) - Baseline `(fc: FeatureContext)`; Target `(fc: BranchStepContext)`.
- `BranchReviewPromptInput` (`prompts/builders/workflow-branch-builder.ts`) - Baseline `readonly prdPath: string`; Target `readonly prdPath?: string`.
- `buildWorkflowBranchFixPrompt` input - Baseline `prdPath: string`; Target `prdPath?: string`.

`FeatureContext` itself does not change. `featureModelFor` and `buildFeatureEnv` keep taking `FeatureContext`.

**Scope** — In: create the narrow types; modify branch-review.ts, branch-fix.ts, repo-gates.ts and workflow-branch-builder.ts as above; add the two new no-PRD test files. This story's tests build a `BranchStepContext` literal by hand, with no `prdPath`. Out: US-002 implements the provider and workflow barrel exports; US-003–US-011 implement forge access. The story-loop, publish/final-gate and verdict schema stay unchanged.

**Interface** — The verbatim interfaces and target signatures above are the contract. `underReviewLimit(sc: Pick<StepContext, "runSignal" | "workflow">, ...)` and `reviewRequiredFiles(paths, fc: Pick<FeatureContext, "config" | "projectDir" | "packageDirs">)` already take slices that `BranchStepContext` satisfies; unchanged.

Verification: the G0 equivalence suites and the existing branch-review, branch-fix, repo-gates and prompt-builder tests run with no edit. New no-PRD tests sit beside them, not inside them; with prdPath present the step and prompt behavior remains byte-identical. In the new no-PRD step test file, also exercise a successful package build followed by a null operation reply: preserve the existing skipped-review result rather than throwing or fabricating a verdict. Use shared test helpers and restore mutable dependency seams after each test.

**Goal** — Build and export a PR-backed branch context without a PRD or nax run record.

**Motivation** — A PR opened by a person has no PRD.

**Approach** — New file `packages/nax/src/workflow/pr-step-context.ts`:
```ts
export interface PrStepContextInput {
  readonly runtime: NaxRuntime;
  /** The config the PR is reviewed under (B2 decides where it loads from). */
  readonly config: NaxConfig;
  /** Root of the checkout that holds the PR head. */
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly agentName: string;
  readonly pr: PrStepRef;
  readonly abortSignal?: AbortSignal;
  readonly interactionChain?: InteractionChain | null;
}

export const _prStepContextDeps = { buildRunDispatchAskWiring };

export async function buildPrStepContext(
  input: PrStepContextInput,
): Promise<{ ctx: PrStepContext; dispose: () => Promise<void> }>;
```

`buildPrStepContext` does, in order:

1. Validate `input.pr`: `number` is a positive integer, `base` and `head` are non-empty strings after trim. Otherwise throw `NaxError` with code `PR_STEP_CONTEXT_INVALID` and context `{ stage: "workflow" }` before anything else runs.
2. `label = "bot-pr-" + number`.
3. Build the ask wiring exactly as `buildFeatureContext` does, through `_prStepContextDeps.buildRunDispatchAskWiring` with `config` and `rootConfig` both `input.config`, `projectDir` and `repoRoot` `input.repoRoot`, `packageDirs: []`, `interaction: input.interactionChain`, `outputDir: input.runtime.outputDir`, `runId: input.runtime.runId`, `featureName: label`, `abortSignal: input.abortSignal`, `stage: "merge"`. The branch fix declares Bash, so the wiring is required.
4. Build `callCtx` with the same shape `buildFeatureContext` builds: `runtime`, `packageView: runtime.packages.resolve(repoRoot)`, `packageDir: repoRoot`, `config`, `agentName`, `featureName: label`, `signal` when an abort signal is given, `askResolver` from the wiring, `commandShadow` when the wiring has one.
5. Return the context with `feature: label`, `projectDir: repoRoot`, `runDir: workflowRunDir(repoRoot, label, runtime.runId)`, `reportPath: join(runDir, "feature-report.md")`, no `prdPath`, `packageDirs: []`, `runSignal` when an abort signal is given, `dryRun: runtime.dryRun === true`, `workflow: resolveWorkflowConfig(config)`, and `pr: input.pr`. `dispose` is the wiring's `dispose`.

No branch step reads `pr`: the review range still comes from the step's `seed` input, so B2 passes `seed = ctx.pr.base`. `pr` is carried for B2's own steps.

The run dir sits inside the checkout under the feature tree on purpose: agents' file tools read review packages from there (nax#2479), and the feature tree's run artifacts are already git-ignored (`NAX_GITIGNORE_ENTRIES`). B2 runs in a throwaway worktree of the PR head, so nothing lands in the user's own checkout. Paths are built with `workflowRunDir` (which goes through `featureDir`), never by hand (`check:feature-dir-ssot`).

`packages/nax/src/workflow/index.ts` (barrel) - Target adds `export type { BranchStepContext, PrStepContext, PrStepRef } from "./branch-step-context"` and `export type { PrStepContextInput } from "./pr-step-context"` and `export { buildPrStepContext } from "./pr-step-context"`.

**Scope** — In: add provider/dependency seam and its test file; update workflow barrel with US-001 and US-002 public types/provider. US-002's integration AC drives the real runBranchReview with a context it built, faking only _workflowDeps.callOp at the operation boundary; use a temporary checkout to exercise the real review-package path. Out: US-001 owns branch-step/prompt narrowing; US-003–US-011 own forge access. No production caller, config selection, checkout creation or run-state persistence is added; those belong to B2.

**Interface** — The verbatim PrStepContextInput, _prStepContextDeps and buildPrStepContext signature above are authoritative; consume the BranchStepContext/PrStepContext/PrStepRef produced by US-001. Ask wiring errors propagate; invalid refs fail before ask wiring. Tests exercise new public symbols through @/workflow and restore injected seams. Existing buildFeatureContext and FeatureContext remain untouched.

**Goal** — Define the forge PR contracts and resolve a numbered PR against the checkout's origin.

**Motivation** — The forge layer can open, view, edit and promote a PR, and post one top-level comment (`packages/nax/src/workflow/promote.ts`). It cannot read review threads or comments, post on a line, reply in a thread, resolve a thread, or tell a fork PR from a trusted one.

**Approach** — **B1 is new forge functions in the existing style**: one function per capability, a `ForgeKind` branch that builds a GitHub argv or a GitLab argv, `deps.run(argv, { cwd })`, a thrown `NaxError` on a non-zero exit (BUG-8 rule, `hasOpenPr` in `forge/pr.ts`), and JSON parsing of stdout. Every read and write goes through the `gh api` / `glab api` REST or GraphQL endpoints with an explicit `--hostname`, never the CLI's implicit repo context. Pagination is nax's own loop over explicit `page` numbers or GraphQL cursors, never the CLI's `--paginate` flag, so a mocked `ForgeDeps` sees every page request.

- `repoSlugFromRemoteUrl(remoteUrl: string): string | null` - the path of an origin URL in both git forms (`git@host:owner/repo.git`, `scheme://[user@]host[:port]/group/sub/proj.git`), with a trailing `.git` and slashes removed; null when no path remains. A port is dropped with the host, as `remoteHost` does.
- `resolvePrRef(number: number, deps: ForgeDeps, cwd: string): Promise<PrRef>` - `detectForge(deps, cwd)`, then `git remote get-url origin`, `remoteHost(url)` for `host` and `repoSlugFromRemoteUrl(url)` for `repo`. A null forge (`detectForge` returned null), a failed remote read, or a null slug throws `NaxError` `FORGE_PR_REF_UNRESOLVED`. A non-positive or non-integer `number` throws the same code before any call.
- `encodeProjectPath(repo: string): string` - `encodeURIComponent(repo)` (`group/proj` -> `group%2Fproj`), the GitLab project id every GitLab endpoint in this feature uses. Every other value interpolated into an endpoint path (logins, ids) also goes through `encodeURIComponent`, so `dependabot[bot]` stays one segment.

Every `NaxError` this feature's forge functions throw carries `stage: "forge"` in its context (`.nax/rules/error-handling.md`).

**Scope** — In: create pure PR types and pr-read.ts with slug/project encoding/reference resolution; export all new types and functions from @/forge; add pr-read.test.ts with injected ForgeDeps. Out: US-004 extends this story's pr-read.ts with readPrInfo; US-005 owns user/trust; US-006/007 own readers; US-008–010 own writes; US-011 owns markers. Origin/upstream detection beyond the specified origin and support for forge host ports are not added.

**Interface** — New file `packages/nax/src/forge/pr-types.ts` (pure types):
```ts
/** One PR on one forge. `repo` is `owner/name` on GitHub, the full project path on GitLab. */
export interface PrRef {
  readonly forge: ForgeKind;
  /** Forge host from the origin remote, passed as --hostname on every call. */
  readonly host: string;
  readonly repo: string;
  readonly number: number;
}

export type PrState = "open" | "closed" | "merged";

export interface PrInfo {
  readonly number: number;
  readonly title: string;
  /** "" when the PR has no description. */
  readonly body: string;
  readonly url: string;
  readonly state: PrState;
  readonly draft: boolean;
  readonly author: { readonly login: string; readonly id?: number };
  readonly baseRef: string;
  readonly headRef: string;
  readonly headSha: string;
  /** True when the head branch lives outside the base repository, or the forge cannot say where. */
  readonly crossRepo: boolean;
  /** GitLab only: the shas an inline comment's position needs. */
  readonly diffRefs?: { readonly baseSha: string; readonly startSha: string; readonly headSha: string };
}

export interface PrTrust {
  readonly trusted: boolean;
  /** The PR head sha this classification was made for (`info.headSha`). A later push needs a new one. */
  readonly headSha: string;
  /** Why, in order of the rules applied. Never empty. */
  readonly reasons: readonly string[];
}

export type PrCommentKind = "thread" | "comment" | "review";

export interface PrComment {
  /** GitHub comment / review id, GitLab note id, as a string. */
  readonly id: string;
  readonly kind: PrCommentKind;
  readonly author: string;
  readonly body: string;
  readonly createdAt: string;
}

export interface PrThread {
  /** GitHub review thread node id, GitLab discussion id: what resolveThread takes. */
  readonly id: string;
  /** GitHub first comment's database id, GitLab discussion id: what replyToThread takes. */
  readonly replyTo: string;
  readonly path?: string;
  readonly line?: number;
  readonly resolved: boolean;
  /** GitHub isOutdated; always false on GitLab. */
  readonly outdated: boolean;
  readonly comments: readonly PrComment[];
}

export interface PostedComment {
  readonly id: string;
  /** GitLab inline comment: the discussion it opened. */
  readonly threadId?: string;
  readonly url?: string;
}

export interface CurrentUser {
  readonly login: string;
  readonly id?: number;
}

export interface OwnComment {
  readonly comment: PrComment;
  /** Set when the comment sits in a review thread. */
  readonly threadId?: string;
  readonly key: string;
}
```

The three function signatures are given verbatim in Approach. Tests import new public symbols through @/forge and assert the argv passed to ForgeDeps.run, not a live forge. Keep existing detectForge and ForgeDeps contracts unchanged.
